From cd98600fa00cd4267f98bc8bc2ee9a8da541c963 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 13:26:58 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] HypreMGR: eliminated points, GPU workaround, refused block
 options

The unknowns which are not kept at a level of reduction are now all
eliminated (HYPRE_MGRSetNonCpointsToFpoints): by default, hypre added
coarse points chosen by an algebraic coarsening at the levels before the
last one. Two levels now need 32 iterations instead of 37 on the host,
41 instead of 87 on a GPU, and converge in single precision, where the
GMRES solver of MFEM reported its convergence with residuals up to 0.8.

When hypre computes on a GPU, the Jacobi relaxation (default) with the
interpolation 12 stopped the program: hypre replaces the relaxation 0 by
its relaxation of the GPU, 7, except with the interpolation 12, which it
then replaces by 2 at the levels eliminating one component, whose Jacobi
relaxation of the host reads the matrices on the GPU. These levels are
now given the relaxation 7.

Refused, after tests on the beam in linear elasticity: the block
interpolations and restrictions (12 to 15) with unknowns ordered by
nodes when a level eliminates several components (hypre groups
consecutive fine unknowns, of different nodes), the interpolation 13
(wrong results), the restriction 12 when a level eliminates several
components (it stops the program), the restrictions 14 and 15 when a
level eliminates one component (invalid values), and, on a GPU, the
interpolation 12 when a level eliminates several components (invalid
memory accesses). The verbosity level is bounded by 7 (the other bits of
the print level of MGR write files), and the size of the operator is
checked on all the processes.

The test checks the block interpolation of two components, the block
interpolation with the Jacobi relaxation, operators of other sizes, one
of them with a process owning no unknown, and the new refusals; the
multigrid reduction must need less than a third of the iterations of
the Jacobi preconditioner. Docs: these rules, the non symmetric default
cycle.
